        "@codesome this is ready for review, please take a look.",
        "cc @bwplotka to weigh in on introducing a cache for matchers->postings for the head block. Can help make ruler queries more efficient as well.",
        "> We could have a global postings cache instead of per query cache. But that comes with some additional memory usage (which can be limited). mimir-prometheus already has a well tested implementation [here](https://github.com/grafana/mimir-prometheus/blob/main/tsdb/postings_for_matchers_cache.go) (and the usage [here](https://github.com/grafana/mimir-prometheus/blob/0db77b4c76da3aa1c17cad8d256887b33a6347a9/tsdb/head_read.go#L123)) that can be considered.\r\n\r\nmimir-prometheus doesn't cache the sorted postings, which may be the most time-consuming part. I don't see the global head cache handling the newly ingested data points(series), which may go against the timeliness. \r\nThe cache entry may be evicted if the timing gets right so the global cache may be not working well in such case.\r\n\r\n> Not all series have the out-of-order data. We could optimize that part so that the out-of-order querier only looks at the series having out of order data. Maybe it is not required for now.\r\n\r\nYou are right and the optimization is already in the code.\r\n",
        "> cc @bwplotka to weigh in on introducing a cache for matchers->postings for the head block. Can help make ruler queries more efficient as well.\r\n\r\nI'm looking forward to this feature.",
        "I think this can co-exist with the global cache since the global cache has to evict some cache entries at some time point ~~, however we can also use some kind of count to avoid the problem~~.",
        "> I think this can co-exist with the global cache since the global cache has to evict some cache entries at some time point ~, however we can also use some kind of count to avoid the problem~.\r\n\r\nIf the cache entry gets evicted, then the global cache can not give a consistent view of the index within a single query. Consistency is another property that this local cache gives. ",
        "> We could have a global postings cache instead of per query cache. But that comes with some additional memory usage (which can be limited). mimir-prometheus already has a well tested implementation [here](https://github.com/grafana/mimir-prometheus/blob/main/tsdb/postings_for_matchers_cache.go) (and the usage [here](https://github.com/grafana/mimir-prometheus/blob/0db77b4c76da3aa1c17cad8d256887b33a6347a9/tsdb/head_read.go#L123)) that can be considered.\r\n\r\nGlobal cache violates the read-after-write guarantee. I think a per-request cache has a better fit in Prometheus, unless we do proper cache invalidation.",
        "Looking at this during the bug scrub.\r\n\r\n@jesusvazquez are you up to reviewing this? It has been a while\u2026 and this seems to be relevant, as far as we can see on the spot.\r\n\r\n@damnever sorry for dropping the ball here. If you don't mind, would you go for a rebase here to resolve conflicts?",
        "@beorn7 rebased, it has taken me some time because the codebase has changed significantly\r\n",
        "@beorn7 Thank you very much.\r\n\r\n@jesusvazquez Do you think you can get to this any time soon?",
        "I have an alternative approach at #14354, which is smaller and faster.\r\n",
        "Closing this since #14354 is merged."
    ],
    "merged": false,
    "created_at": "2023-12-12T11:20:38Z",
    "issue_context": {
        "number": 11628,
        "title": "Reuse the postings index when head and ooo head are overlapped",
        "body": "### Proposal\r\n\r\nCurrently, the head index and the ooo head index are the same, when both head and ooo head fall into the same query range, we can reuse the postings index to avoid duplicate computation and reduce memory usage.",
        "comments": [
            "Do you have a benchmark that illustrates the problem?",
            "I am talking about this in theory, the [PostingsForMatchers](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/30b31cae93b2fe66486f188c07e215b80ebc93f8/tsdb/querier.go#L228) will be executed twice if both heads fall into the same query range.\r\n\r\nI would like to hear more constructive input before I write any code.",
            "My constructive feedback is to show a benchmark. ",
            "@bboreham I can't follow you, and with my limited experience, I am hoping you explain the \"benchmark\" a little bit, is there anything we can talk about before a real benchmark? Ideas, experiences, and numbers in theory could be helpful.",
            "@bboreham As you wish, I have submitted #11644 with a benchmark to illustrate the problem, I hope that you find it useful, and maybe give some advice and ideas based on the patch.",
            "I have another idea, which I shall call `ActiveQueryPostingsCache`. This cache will be available only to active(running) queries. However, there may be abnormal queries that remain active indefinitely. For such situations, we need to enforce a duration of validity. We modify the `Querier.Select(...) XSet` to `Querier.Select(...) (XSet, func())` to conduct appropriate cleanup work. The cleanup process will remove the cache entry when the reference count decreases to zero, ensuring that the postings cache remains valid during the entire lifetime of that query. As stated before, we will enforce a duration of validity to prevent caches from retaining their validity for an excessive amount of time.\r\n",
            "> abnormal queries that remain active indefinitely\r\n\r\nCan you explain how this happens?",
            "> > abnormal queries that remain active indefinitely\r\n> \r\n> Can you explain how this happens?\r\n\r\nPerhaps I should quote the 'abnormal'.\r\n\r\nSome clients may periodically send identical queries, such as recording rules. Assuming the interval is 5 seconds, if the query execution exceeds this duration, the postings cache from the head may remain valid indefinitely, thus causing problems.\r\n\r\n",
            "Thanks for creating a benchmark. From profiling it seems that most of the work is in merging two sets of series.  And in fact they are actually the same set of series.\r\n\r\nThis led me to think of another way to solve the problem: create an iterator that walks the series for both head and OOO-head, and merges just the chunks.  This would avoid any cache lifetime issues.\r\n\r\n[Returning to this because I noticed a related issue in a production system at work - it spent a lot of time executing regexps, and it did the same work for head and ooo-head.]",
